From: ADIOS scheduler analysis
Subject: [PATCH] adios: v3.3.5-SAGA -- 3 fixes from a third external
 technical review, each independently verified against the real 6.1
 tree before applying (not applied on trust)

This review (a standalone document, not a chat-interactive round like
the previous two) raised 24 points. Only the 3 below were verified
real and applied here. The rest were either read and judged to be
design opinions/lower-severity suggestions not independently verified
against source before this patch, one was verified and found to
already be handled (see below), and one was verified and found to be
a false alarm (also below) -- neither of those two needed a code
change.

1. REAL, most serious: cross-CPU race in the latency model's per-CPU
   bucket reset. latency_model_input() (hot-path writer, called on
   every IO completion) only ever protects its own bucket with
   local_irq_save() on the CPU it's executing on -- that provides zero
   exclusion against a *different* CPU touching the same memory.
   lm_reset_pcpu_buckets() -- called from sideload_latency_model(), the
   rare sysfs-triggered reset path -- iterated every possible CPU with
   a raw memset(), while holding only model->update_lock, which the
   writer never takes. A completion on CPU X racing a reset from CPU Y
   could corrupt sum_of_weights/weighted_sum_latency mid read-modify-
   write, and on the next delta-vs-snapshot computation a zeroed
   "current" against a stale nonzero "snapshot" can underflow a u64 by
   a large margin, feeding garbage into the regression, confidence
   gate, and shrink logic.
   Fix: lm_reset_pcpu_buckets() now uses on_each_cpu() to run the reset
   on each ONLINE cpu with that cpu's own irqs disabled (standard SMP
   call-function context) -- the same discipline the writer already
   uses on itself, so the two can never interleave on the same CPU.
   Offline CPUs can't be running the writer either, so they don't need
   an IPI. Only touches the rare explicit-reset path; the hot path
   (latency_model_input itself) is completely unchanged, so this adds
   zero per-completion cost.

2. REAL, minor: u64->u32 sysfs truncation. batch_limit[]'s store
   function parsed input with kstrtoul() into an `unsigned long`
   (64-bit on arm64) with only a "!= 0" check -- no upper bound --
   then assigned it into batch_limit[], which is u32. A value like
   4294967296 parses fine and silently becomes 0 on assignment.
   Checked every other kstrtoul() call site in this file first: all of
   them either target a genuinely u64 field (matches unsigned long
   exactly, no truncation possible), are used only as a boolean
   trigger (never stored), or already have an explicit min/max range
   check that keeps the value within the target field's width
   regardless of kstrtoul's 64 bits -- batch_limit's store was the only
   one with neither a matching width nor a bound. Fixed by switching
   just that one to kstrtou32(), which rejects out-of-range input
   outright (-ERANGE) instead of relying on an unwritten range check.

3. REAL, scope issue: the boot-time "adios" enforcer (genhd.c,
   saga_adios_enforce_fn(), late_initcall, added to win a race against
   vendor init possibly setting a different scheduler after boot) was
   calling elv_iosched_store(q, "adios", 5) on every disk in the
   system (block_class / disk_type, no filter beyond nonzero capacity)
   -- 5 times, 20s apart, so anywhere from ~20s to ~100s into boot.
   That's broader than the problem it's solving: it can briefly
   perturb queues that have nothing to do with the device actually
   being tuned (boot/vendor/cache/other LUs) during the most
   IO-sensitive part of boot. The reviewer's blanket "remove the whole
   mechanism" suggestion isn't quite right either -- it exists for a
   real, previously-observed reason (vendor init overriding the
   scheduler post-boot) and removing it reopens that. Went with the
   reviewer's own second suggestion instead: new Kconfig string
   MQ_IOSCHED_ADIOS_ENFORCE_DISK lets the builder restrict enforcement
   to one disk_name (e.g. "sdc" -- note this can differ from what
   you'd guess, and even between devices/firmware on the same phone
   model). Empty string, the default, preserves the exact original
   unrestricted behavior -- so this is opt-in, not a behavior change
   for anyone who doesn't set it.

Verified but NOT applied (checked against real source, found to be a
non-issue):

- The review's top "P0" item claimed guard()/scoped_guard() (used
  throughout this file, e.g. scoped_guard(spinlock_irqsave, ...))
  might not be available on a vanilla Linux 6.1 tree and should be
  removed/replaced. Checked the actual android14-6.1-lts source
  directly: include/linux/cleanup.h has the full guard()/scoped_guard()
  macro infrastructure, and include/linux/spinlock.h has
  DEFINE_LOCK_GUARD_1(spinlock_irqsave, ...) specifically -- the exact
  variant this file uses. Present natively, nothing to change.

- The review's Section 1 ("critical problem") claimed the depth
  controller compares true per-request latency against a prediction
  trained on a different metric (inter-completion gap), and recommends
  splitting them into two separate models. Checked the code: they're
  already split, from an earlier review round -- see the
  "CHATGPT-REVIEW FIX #3" comment already present above true_latency's
  definition, which explains explicitly why the inter-completion
  metric (`latency`) stays feeding the model's own regression while
  the depth controller and overrun detection use only true_latency
  (now - rq->io_start_time_ns). Nothing to change; the review appears
  to be describing a state the code was already moved past.

Not independently verified against source in this round (read and
judged plausible-to-debatable, not confirmed real or ruled out by
inspection): double-buffer residual window in the adaptive depth
controller, confidence-gate blind spot to workload-shape changes,
first-seen-TGID interactive boost heuristic, batch_actual_max_* stats
synchronization, force_shrink_model's rcu_head-copy style,
exit_sched() invariant coverage, and the --fuzz=3 default in
adios.sh's own apply step (this last one is real and known -- see
adios-topapp's own script, which already uses --fuzz=0 as a guard for
exactly this reason -- but changing adios.sh's tolerance is a build-
process decision, not a code fix, and wasn't part of what was asked
for in this pass).

Verified: `patch -p1 --fuzz=0` against a freshly reconstructed
block/adios.c (prior v3.3.5 state applied first) -- zero rejects,
zero fuzz, zero offset on Kconfig.iosched, adios.c, and genhd.c (the
three files this patch actually touches).
