From: ADIOS scheduler analysis
Subject: [PATCH] adios: v3.3.5-SAGA -- 2 more fixes from the same
 external review, after checking every remaining unverified item
 against source

Continued auditing the same review document's remaining 21 points
against the real file. Result: several were already fixed in earlier
review rounds (documented below), one is a genuine but low-severity
gap now fixed, one design critique is valid but deliberately left for
future work, and the rest are style/organizational opinions with no
code change warranted.

4. REAL, low severity: batch_actual_max_size[]/batch_actual_max_total
   (pure observability counters -- "biggest batch ever dispatched",
   never read back into any scheduling decision) are updated in
   fill_batch_queues(), called only from dispatch_from_bq() under
   ad->bq_lock -- so the hot-path write side is already correctly
   locked. But adios_batch_actual_max_show() (sysfs read) and
   adios_reset_bq_stats_store() (sysfs reset) accessed the same fields
   with no lock at all. Fixed by wrapping both in
   scoped_guard(spinlock_irqsave, &ad->bq_lock) -- rare, non-hot-path
   sysfs calls, so the extra lock/unlock is free in practice. Worst
   case before this fix was a torn read of a debug counter or a reset
   racing a concurrent max-update and losing one sample -- cosmetic,
   never fed back into scheduling, but the fix was nearly free so
   applied anyway.

14. REAL, cheap hardening: exit_sched() only WARN_ON_ONCE'd that
   prio_queue[0/1] were empty at teardown -- nothing checked dl_tree[]
   (the deadline-sorted RB-trees) or batch_queue[][] (the batch-queue
   lists). Scheduler switching (none <-> adios <-> mq-deadline, which
   this same file's own genhd.c enforcer can trigger) is exactly where
   a request left behind in a structure teardown doesn't inspect would
   surface as a silent leak instead of a loud, diagnosable warning.
   Added the same WARN_ON_ONCE pattern for both: RB_EMPTY_ROOT() on
   both dl_tree[] roots, and list_empty() across every
   batch_queue[page][optype] combination. Diagnostic only -- doesn't
   change behavior when everything actually is empty (the expected
   case), just gives a specific WARN_ON_ONCE hit instead of silence if
   a future change leaks a request into either structure.

Already fixed in earlier review rounds (re-verified against current
source, no new change needed):

- Item 3 / P1.2 (double-buffer residual race in the adaptive depth
  controller): the struct's own comment, marked "CHATGPT-REVIEW FIX #1
  v2", already documents this exact scenario -- a writer that read the
  old buffer index microseconds before an evaluator's cmpxchg-swap can
  still land one sample in the buffer being evaluated. It's bounded
  and explicitly accepted: "a single-sample-per-window skew at most,
  standard tradeoff for lock-free double-buffered kernel stats vs. a
  full seqlock" -- not an overlooked bug, a reasoned tradeoff already
  documented where it lives.
- Item 12 (confidence gate blind to workload-shape change): partially
  already addressed by "CHATGPT-REVIEW FIX #2" -- model_is_confident()
  checks the SAME bucket (small vs large) the current request will
  actually use, not an unrelated one. The review's deeper point (raw
  sample count within the right bucket still doesn't capture *recent*
  prediction error/variance/model staleness) is valid and NOT
  addressed -- left as a real, standalone future enhancement (would
  need a recent-error-window mechanism, not a quick fix), not folded
  into this patch.

Read, judged to be valid design opinions or lower-priority
observations, no code change made:

- Item 9 (READ_ONCE()/WRITE_ONCE() audit): checked the specific fields
  the review names -- batch_limit[], latency_target[],
  global_latency_window, lm_block_size_threshold. Confirmed real: none
  of them use READ_ONCE/WRITE_ONCE on either the sysfs-store or hot-
  path-read side (unlike global_latency_window/compliance_flags'
  SYSFS_ULL_DECL macro, which does). This is a legitimate gap, but a
  systematic fix means auditing every tunable's every read site
  individually, and the reviewer's own closing note says READ_ONCE
  alone doesn't even solve cases needing multi-field consistency
  (those need a lock/seqcount instead) -- sprinkling it on a handful of
  named fields without that full audit risks false confidence more
  than it helps. Treating as separate future work, not bolted onto
  this patch.
- Item 11 (first-seen-TGID interactive boost is too eager): a genuine
  design suggestion (require "recently idle/foreground", not just
  "first IO from this TGID"), not a bug -- no crash or corruption
  results from the current heuristic, just a possibly-too-generous
  boost. Left for a future, separate change.
- Item 13 (force_shrink_model's rcu_head-copy style): the review
  itself doesn't call this a serious bug ("Não considero esse ponto um
  bug grave"), just a readability preference for explicit field-by-
  field copying over copying the whole struct including its rcu_head.
  No functional issue -- skipped.
- Item 15 (patch is too large as a single unit / should be split into
  conceptual layers): a build-process/maintainability opinion about
  how this addon is organized, not a defect in what it does. Noted,
  not acted on here.
- The --fuzz=3 default in adios.sh's own apply step: real and already
  known (adios-topapp's own former script used --fuzz=0 specifically
  to guard against this same class of silent-corruption risk after an
  earlier incident in this project). Changing adios.sh's tolerance is
  a build-process decision, not a code fix, and wasn't asked for in
  this pass.

Verified: `patch -p1 --fuzz=0` against a freshly reconstructed
block/adios.c (prior v3.3.5 state applied first) -- zero rejects,
zero fuzz, zero offset on Kconfig.iosched, adios.c, and genhd.c.
