From: ADIOS scheduler analysis
Subject: [PATCH] adios: v3.3.5-SAGA -- close item 9 (READ_ONCE/
 WRITE_ONCE consistency) for the specific fields it named

Previously deferred as "real but needs a full audit, not a spot fix."
Did that audit: grep'd every plain (non-ONCE) field access, cross-
referenced against every READ_ONCE(ad->.../model->...) already in the
file to find the existing pattern, then applied it to exactly the
fields the review named plus one the audit caught that the review
didn't:

- topapp_deadline_bonus: found this was MY OWN inconsistency from the
  earlier bore/adios-topapp work -- the self-activation timer already
  wrote it with WRITE_ONCE, but the hot-path read in add_to_dl_tree()
  was plain. Fixed the read side to match what the write side already
  did.
- latency_target[]: hot-path read (add_to_dl_tree) now READ_ONCE, the
  sysfs store now WRITE_ONCE.
- global_latency_window: the two hot-path reads (fill_batch_queues,
  dispatch_from_bq) now READ_ONCE. Its sysfs write already used
  WRITE_ONCE (SYSFS_ULL_DECL macro). Its *other* write site --
  adios_init_sched()'s one-time rotational/queue-depth heuristic --
  was deliberately left alone: it runs once, single-threaded, before
  the struct is published to any other CPU, so there's no concurrent
  reader to race with at that point; adding ONCE there would be
  cosmetic, not a fix for anything.
- lm_block_size_threshold: the sysfs store already took
  model->update_lock, but that only protects against a second
  concurrent *writer* -- the 3 hot-path readers (latency_model_input,
  predict latency, and the small/large count lookup) are
  *deliberately* lock-free by design (that's the whole point of the
  per-CPU bucket architecture), so the writer's lock gives a lock-free
  reader zero benefit on its own. Added WRITE_ONCE on the (still
  locked, for the other reason) write, and READ_ONCE on all 3 reads,
  so writer and lock-free readers are at least each individually
  well-defined instead of the compiler being free to assume no one
  else is looking.

Scope check: confirmed via grep that the depth-controller's own family
of tunables (lm_adapt_*, already 13 READ_ONCE + a few WRITE_ONCE call
sites) already followed this discipline consistently -- this patch
brings the remaining flagged fields in line with that existing
pattern, rather than inventing a new one.

Not touched, and shouldn't be: fields that are only ever read/written
under a lock both sides (e.g. the depth-controller's window state,
already protected end-to-end) don't need READ_ONCE/WRITE_ONCE on top
of the lock -- adding it there would be noise, not a fix. Multi-field
invariants (the review's own closing caveat) still aren't addressed by
any of this -- READ_ONCE/WRITE_ONCE only ever makes a single access
well-defined, never a relationship between two fields; none of the
fields touched here have that kind of cross-field dependency, so
plain per-field ONCE annotation was the right-sized fix in every case
here.

Verified: `patch -p1 --fuzz=0` against a freshly reconstructed
block/adios.c (prior v3.3.5 state applied first) -- zero rejects,
zero fuzz, zero offset.
