From: ADIOS scheduler analysis
Subject: [PATCH] adios: v3.3.5-SAGA -- 3 findings from a second,
 independent external review, one of them a real regression this
 patch's own earlier fix introduced

This review explicitly scoped itself to items NOT already covered by
prior rounds documented in this file, and found something none of the
earlier rounds (including the ones in this same file) caught: the
per-CPU race fix earlier in this changelog introduced a worse bug than
the one it fixed. All 3 below verified against real source (this
tree's kernel/smp.c for #1, this file for #2 and #3) before applying.

1. CRITICAL, own regression: sideload_latency_model() calls
   spin_lock_irqsave(&model->update_lock, flags) -- disabling this
   CPU's local IRQs -- and, still inside that section, called
   lm_reset_pcpu_buckets(), which (per this same v3.3.5's earlier fix)
   is on_each_cpu(fn, model, 1), i.e. wait=1. Checked this tree's own
   kernel/smp.c: smp_call_function_many_cond() (which on_each_cpu()
   uses) asserts lockdep_assert_irqs_enabled() and documents why in
   its own comment -- "Can deadlock when called with interrupts
   disabled". This is reachable from userspace (any sysfs write that
   triggers a sideload -- e.g. lm_block_size_threshold's store, or
   direct reset), not a theoretical path. Fix: moved the
   lm_reset_pcpu_buckets(model) call to before spin_lock_irqsave()
   instead of after it -- ordinary IRQs-enabled task-context footing,
   same as any other on_each_cpu() caller, same as it should have been
   the first time. This does introduce a benign ordering skew (a
   completion racing this exact call could land in a freshly-zeroed
   bucket against the about-to-be-replaced old params instead of the
   also-about-to-be-replaced old bucket state) -- explicitly accepted
   in the new comment at the call site, same class of single-sample
   skew this file already accepts elsewhere (e.g. the depth
   controller's double-buffer, documented in its own struct comment).
   Lesson noted: the fix for the original per-CPU race was verified by
   confirming it applies and compiles cleanly, but not checked against
   every calling *context* it would run in -- braces balancing and a
   clean patch apply don't catch a locking-context violation like this
   one. Worth being more suspicious of exactly this class of thing
   going forward.

2. Cosmetic, no functional impact: a comment directly above
   shrink_work_fn() still described force_shrink_model()'s allocation
   as "still GFP_ATOMIC ... left as GFP_ATOMIC deliberately" -- stale
   since an earlier review round ("SAGA FIX 3rd review round",
   documented right above force_shrink_model() itself) already moved
   that allocation to GFP_KERNEL, before the lock. The two comments
   simply never got reconciled when the second one was written. Fixed
   the stale one to describe the actual current code instead of
   contradicting it -- orphaned comments like this are exactly what
   caused this same review to have to go re-verify something already
   fixed elsewhere in the file.

3. REAL, architectural gap: block/elevator.c's
   elevator_get_default(), CONFIG_MQ_IOSCHED_DEFAULT_ADIOS branch,
   returned elevator_get(q, "adios", false) unconditionally --
   completely skipping the hardware-topology guard
   (q->nr_hw_queues != 1 && !blk_mq_is_shared_tags(...) -> NULL) that
   the stock/mq-deadline branch right below it still respects. That
   guard exists upstream specifically to avoid forcing a software
   scheduler onto a queue with genuine multiple hardware queues and no
   shared tags -- real hardware-level parallelism that software
   reordering doesn't help and can actively hurt. This backport
   explicitly targets UFS MCQ, which is exactly a multiple-hw-queue
   configuration, so this gap could force ADIOS onto precisely the
   kind of queue upstream's own logic says not to. Not mentioned in
   any prior review round. Fixed by applying the identical guard check
   in the ADIOS branch before calling elevator_get() -- ADIOS gets no
   special exemption from a check that exists for a hardware reason,
   not a scheduler-specific one.

Verified but not independently re-confirmed in this round (this
review's own "verified correct" list, taken on the reviewer's word
rather than re-derived from source again here): batch_limit[]'s
kstrtou32 fix, lm_interactive_boost_ns's SYSFS_ULL_DECL fix, and
lm_adapt_bounds_lock's 4-attribute TOCTOU serialization -- all three
were already fixed/verified in earlier rounds in this same file and
this review found nothing new to add on any of them.

Verified: `patch -p1 --fuzz=0` against a freshly reconstructed
block/adios.c (prior v3.3.5 state applied first) -- zero rejects,
zero fuzz, zero offset on Kconfig.iosched, adios.c, elevator.c, and
