From: ADIOS scheduler analysis
Subject: [PATCH] adios: v3.3.5-SAGA -- a full, unscoped review pass
 (14 findings), every one individually verified against real source
 before acting

Unlike prior rounds in this file, this review was not scoped to "what
hasn't been checked yet" -- it re-examined the whole patch from
scratch. Went through all 14 findings one at a time; each verified
result below states exactly what was checked and against what. 8 were
real and got fixed, 3 were false alarms once checked against this
specific tree, 1 needs a tuning decision this patch can't make alone,
1 was already correct, 1 is a real gap outside this patch's scope.

REAL, fixed:

1. Priority inversion in dispatch_from_pq(): pq_idx = pq_state >> 1
   gave 3 >> 1 == 1 when both prio_queue[0] (Tier 0,
   BLK_MQ_INSERT_AT_HEAD) and prio_queue[1] (pre-model-stability
   fallback) were non-empty -- picking the lower-priority queue while
   a Tier-0 request waited. Traced both insert sites to confirm which
   index means what before fixing: prio_queue[1] is only ever used
   during the brief pre-stability window early in device life (once
   both READ/WRITE models get a first sample, nothing inserts into it
   again), so this could only manifest in that narrow window, not as
   an ongoing steady-state problem -- still a real, confirmed logic
   bug with a trivial fix: pq_idx = (pq_state & 1) ? 0 : 1.

2. .next_request/.former_request (elv_rb_latter_request/
   elv_rb_former_request) operate on rq->rb_node directly, assuming
   one rb-tree node per request -- ADIOS's dl_tree groups requests by
   struct dl_group (one node per distinct deadline, requests chained
   via rd->dl_node, a plain list) and never touches rq->rb_node at
   all. Traced the actual call path: block/blk-merge.c's
   attempt_back_merge()/attempt_front_merge() call
   elv_latter_request()/elv_former_request() directly, independent of
   ADIOS's own .bio_merge/.request_merged/.requests_merged -- so this
   is reachable, not dead code, and would read stale/garbage
   rq->rb_node contents. Confirmed elv_latter_request/elv_former_
   request (block/elevator.c) already null-check
   ops.next_request/.former_request before calling, so removing both
   assignments is a safe, zero-risk fix -- that one generic merge
   fallback becomes a no-op, ADIOS's own real merge logic is
   unaffected.

3. fill_batch_queues() classified sync vs async by comparing
   rd->deadline against rq->start_time_ns -- but interactive-boost and
   the top-app deadline bonus (this file's own earlier addition) both
   subtract from rd->deadline for genuinely sync requests, so a
   boosted sync read ends up misclassified as async (the "can be
   delayed freely" tier) -- exactly backwards from what the boost is
   for. Added an explicit is_sync bool to adios_rq_data, set once from
   rq->cmd_flags & REQ_SYNC before anything touches rd->deadline, used
   instead of the deadline comparison.

4. adios_prepare_request(): mempool_alloc(..., GFP_ATOMIC) has no
   guarantee of success, fed straight into memset(rd, 0, ...) with no
   NULL check -- an immediate crash under exactly the memory pressure
   this scheduler's own shrink logic exists to survive. Added the
   NULL check. Explicitly NOT a complete fix: get_rq_data() is a raw
   pointer return with no NULL check, and roughly 8 call sites
   dereference its result immediately, so a request left with
   priv[0] == NULL here still crashes a few lines later in the insert
   path instead of right here. A real fix needs either auditing every
   get_rq_data() call site or pre-allocating per-tag private data at
   init time (bounded by q->nr_requests, never fails) instead of a
   mempool -- both bigger, separate pieces of work.

5. CONFIG_MQ_IOSCHED_ADIOS is tristate (can be =m), but
   subsys_initcall(adios_init) was unconditional -- the module loader
   doesn't process initcall sections at all, so a =m build would
   insmod "successfully" with elv_register() never called. Wrapped in
   #ifdef MODULE / module_init() / #else / subsys_initcall() / #endif,
   keeping the existing UFS-MTK probe-race comment scoped to the =y
   branch it actually applies to (a =m build can't win that race
   regardless of initcall level, since modules load after every
   built-in initcall has already run).

10. update_elv_direction() in pop_next_bq_request_elevator() checked
    `bq_idx == 0 && !(bq_state & 0x1)` using bq_state captured BEFORE
    the pop -- but bq_idx == 0 requires bit 0 already set in that same
    snapshot (__builtin_ctz found it), so `bq_state & 0x1` was always
    true whenever bq_idx == 0, making the condition impossible to
    satisfy. Confirmed pop_bq_request() clears the bit in
    ad->bq_state[page] directly when the pop empties the queue -- so
    re-reading ad->bq_state[ad->bq_page] (post-pop, live) instead of
    the stale local snapshot is the fix.

11. Three more sysfs tunables read in a hot path without READ_ONCE
    (lm_adapt_window_samples, lm_overrun_ratio_pct,
    lm_overrun_trigger_count) -- missed in this file's earlier
    READ_ONCE/WRITE_ONCE audit round, a different family of tunables
    than the ones checked then. Write side was already WRITE_ONCE'd in
    all three cases (SYSFS_INT_DECL's generic macro already does
    this) -- only the reads needed fixing. (The fourth field this
    review named, lm_adapt_bad_threshold_pct, was checked and found to
    already have READ_ONCE at its actual use site -- false alarm on
    that one specifically.)

14. adios_batch_limit_##name##_store(): struct adios_data *ad =
    e->elevator_data was declared after the kstrtou32()/range-check
    statements -- valid C, but mixed declarations-and-code, which can
    trigger -Wdeclaration-after-statement on stricter toolchain
    configs. Purely cosmetic, zero functional risk -- moved the
    declaration to the top.

7 (partial, real gap found beyond what the review itself flagged):
   elv_iosched_store() (block/elevator.c) is non-static so it links,
   but checked every header genhd.c would plausibly already include
   (block/blk.h, include/linux/elevator.h) and found no prototype for
   it anywhere -- calling it from genhd.c's enforcer with no visible
   declaration is an implicit-function-declaration situation, which
   real toolchains (Clang especially) can treat as a hard build error,
   not just a warning. Added a local extern declaration in the same
   genhd.c block rather than editing a shared header this patch
   otherwise never touches. (q->sysfs_lock being a mutex, and
   elv_iosched_store's own locking expectations, were also part of
   this item -- checked and confirmed fine, see below.)

REAL, but needs a decision this patch can't make alone:

12. The batch_limit auto-scale block's own comment says it targets
    "the batch_limit=128-on-a-QD32-device regression observed
    empirically on UFS 2.2" -- but baseline_qd is 32, so for a device
    with q->nr_requests == 32 (exactly the case named), scale_permille
    computes to exactly 1000 (1.0x), leaving batch_limit[WRITE] at its
    full default of 128, completely unscaled. The math only changes
    anything for devices *other* than the one the comment says it
    fixes. This means either baseline_qd is wrong (should be some
    higher value representing the depth 128 was actually tuned for,
    so a QD32 device scales down from it) or the default itself needs
    revisiting -- not something to guess a replacement number for
    without knowing what depth 128 was empirically validated against.
    Left as-is pending that input.

Checked, confirmed already correct or a false alarm for this tree
specifically (no change made):

6. guard()/scoped_guard() -- re-confirmed already verified true in an
   earlier round of this same file's changelog (real, present natively
   in this tree's include/linux/cleanup.h and spinlock.h).
8. req_get_ioprio(), blk_mq_is_shared_tags(q->tag_set->flags), and
   QUEUE_FLAG_SQ_SCHED -- all three checked directly against this
   tree's real headers/source (include/linux/blk-mq.h,
   include/linux/blkdev.h, and blk_mq_is_shared_tags's real call sites
   in block/elevator.c itself, block/blk-mq.c, block/blk-mq-sched.c).
   All exist with the exact signatures already used. False alarm.
13. RQF_STARTED set manually in adios_dispatch_request(), suspected
    redundant with blk_mq_start_request(). Checked this tree's actual
    blk_mq_start_request() (block/blk-mq.c): it does NOT set
    RQF_STARTED at all in this kernel version -- it uses a newer,
    separate mechanism (WRITE_ONCE(rq->state, MQ_RQ_IN_FLIGHT)).
    RQF_STARTED is legacy/vestigial for this code path here; setting
    it manually isn't redundant with anything blk_mq_start_request()
    does in this tree, and may be relied on by something else that
    still checks that specific flag. The review's concern appears
    based on a different kernel version's blk_mq_start_request()
    behavior. False alarm for this tree.
9. Truncation on 32-bit (kstrtoul into unsigned long, 32-bit on arm32)
   -- correctly caveated by the review itself as conditional on
   32-bit being a real target. This kernel/device family is arm64
   only; not applicable.

Verified: `patch -p1 --fuzz=0` against a freshly reconstructed
block/adios.c (prior v3.3.5 state applied first) -- zero rejects,
zero fuzz, zero offset on Kconfig.iosched, adios.c, elevator.c, and
genhd.c.
