From: ADIOS Clutch analysis
Subject: [PATCH] adios: SAGA-Clutch variant -- prep for the future
 Cerebral Clutch CPU governor, per a dedicated review of what a CPU
 governor consumer would need from this file

This is a SEPARATE variant, not a replacement for the "adios" addon's
own v3.3.5-SAGA -- both create block/adios.c from scratch and can't
coexist in the same build (see adios-clutch.sh's own guard). Meant to
be swapped in/out via ADDONS= for side-by-side testing.

Fixed (real, verified against source):

- sideload_latency_model() previously left ad->aggr_buckets reset up
  to each of its 5 callers individually -- only 2 of the 5
  (adios_lat_model_*_store, adios_reset_lat_model_store's mode-2
  branch) actually called reset_buckets(ad->aggr_buckets) afterward,
  and both did so as a separate, unlocked step after the function had
  already returned (a completion landing in that gap would compute
  against the new params but aggregate into the stale, pre-reset
  buckets). The other 3 callers (lm_target's store, reset_lat_model's
  mode-1 branch, lm_block_size_threshold's store) didn't reset
  aggr_buckets at all. Centralized the reset inside
  sideload_latency_model() itself now, under the same
  model->update_lock that already protects aggr_buckets in
  latency_model_update() -- every sideload path is consistent now,
  atomically, not just some of them.

New, read-only sysfs interface (for an external consumer, e.g. a
future CPU governor -- not just human debugging):

- in_flight_stats: count + total_pred_lat_ns from the existing
  in_flight_rqs atomic counter (already maintained internally by
  fill_batch_queues()/dispatch_from_bq(); this just exposes it).
- confidence: per-optype small/large-regime confidence booleans, same
  threshold model_is_confident() already checks against internally.
- io_pressure: 0-100 score = how much of global_latency_window's own
  budget is currently outstanding (total_pred_lat / window * 100,
  capped at 100) -- the exact ratio dispatch_from_bq() already
  computes to decide batch-queue refill timing, just exposed rather
  than reinvented. Deliberately does NOT walk dl_tree/batch_queue to
  count individually pending requests -- no existing cheap counter for
  that, and adding one is a bigger, separate change; in_flight_rqs
  already captures what this scheduler itself treats as "outstanding"
  for the same refill-timing purpose, so it's the grounded thing to
  reuse rather than invent a fresh heuristic.

Checked and NOT changed, with reasoning (matches the analysis this
patch was built from, all independently re-verified against source
here, not taken on that document's word):

- batch_limit auto-scale (documented pending item from the previous
  review round): still no data on what queue depth the WRITE default
  of 128 was actually calibrated against, so no number was guessed.
  Left as-is, still flagged as an open question.
- lm_adapt_step_permille vs lm_adapt_step_down_permille asymmetry: the
  analysis that prompted this patch asked for a comment explaining the
  +5%/-10% asymmetric step. Checked: a "CHATGPT-REVIEW" comment
  already sits directly above these two defaults explaining exactly
  this ("expand cautiously, contract fast... standard control-theory
  technique to avoid overshoot"). Already done; false alarm on this
  specific point.
- kfree_rcu(old_params, rcu) without a NULL check: checked this tree's
  real include/linux/rcupdate.h -- kvfree_rcu_arg_2's expansion already
  wraps its body in `if (___p) { ... }`, so kfree_rcu on a NULL pointer
  is already a safe no-op by the macro's own definition. No fix
  needed.
- get_rq_data()/mempool_alloc() NULL-safety (pre-allocate per-tag data
  instead of a mempool, so allocation can never fail): checked the
  actual pool sizing and free pairing first. ad->rq_data_pool is
  created via mempool_create_slab_pool(q->nr_requests, ...) -- exactly
  the maximum number of requests this queue can ever have in flight
  simultaneously (bounded by available tags) -- and every allocated
  rd is freed back via mempool_free() in .finish_request, which the
  block layer guarantees runs before a tag (and thus its rd) can be
  reused for a new request. Given that pairing, the pool's reserve
  can't be exhausted by legitimate concurrent use: as many rd's as
  there are in-flight requests, never more, and the pool already holds
  that many. The NULL check added in an earlier review round remains
  as defense-in-depth, but the bigger pre-allocated-array redesign
  this document proposed would add real implementation risk for a
  failure mode that's already structurally prevented by the existing
  pool sizing -- not pursued.
- effective_batch_limit()'s max_t(u64, 1, effective): the literal `1`
  (int) does get promoted correctly against a u64 in max_t's
  comparison, this was purely a style nit. Changed to `1ULL` for
  clarity; no functional difference.

adios_version now reports "3.3.5-SAGA-Clutch" (was "3.3.5-SAGA") so
sysfs can distinguish which variant is running.

Verified: `patch -p1 --fuzz=0` against a freshly reconstructed
block/adios.c (base v3.3.5-SAGA content applied first) -- zero
rejects, zero fuzz, zero offset on Kconfig.iosched, adios.c,
elevator.c, and genhd.c.
